{"connection_id": "BC-bcd2896411e09fdc833e", "ref": "Didache 1:1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two ways, life and death", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the path of those favored by God from the paths of wrath and straying; the Didache opens its instruction with two ways, one of life and one of death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-72de0e0dc2e3b19c3827", "ref": "Didache 7:1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "baptism in living water", "note": "The Didache prefers baptism in living water. Its ritual preference turns running or fresh water into a sign of new life, extending the commentary's literal contrast between a life-giving source and lost water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d1cae7b77817fdfa8502", "ref": "Epistle of Barnabas 18:1–20:2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "way of light and way of darkness", "note": "The commentary asks for the straight path rather than the paths of wrath and straying; Barnabas sets out a way of light against a way of darkness, a Christian ethical expansion of the two-way contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e98b6aaa459aef6ad4c", "ref": "Origen, Homilies on Genesis 13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Isaac's wells read as scripture", "note": "Origen's homily on Isaac's reopened wells treats digging wells as recovering the deep waters of Scripture. The reading gives a Christian interpretive afterlife to the commentary's well as a source that must be reached and kept open."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a4db385a10b85d2c2408", "ref": "Peshitta, John 3:16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Syriac ʿālmā, world", "note": "The commentary labels al-ʿālamīn from ʿ-L-M and also develops a deep, water-filled well from that family; the Peshitta uses cognate ʿālmā for “world,” showing the word’s spatial world sense in a Christian text."}
{"connection_id": "BC-612373a59d6933225b30", "ref": "Protevangelium of James 11:1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Mary goes to draw water", "note": "In the usual chapter division, Mary goes out with a water jar and hears the annunciatory greeting. This Christian apocryphal well-side encounter belongs beside the commentary's women at water, where drawing water becomes a setting for divine gift."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5807b09f90e00dee4729", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cloud over the wilderness journey", "note": "The commentary brings cloud, travel, and water together; Paul recalls Israel’s ancestors under the cloud and on their wilderness passage, supplying the Christian pilgrimage setting for his water reference."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2507e0f30312ec6be421", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cloud over a travelling people", "note": "Israel's wilderness journey takes place under the cloud. Together with the water in verse 4, this creates a Christian rereading in which cloud and water accompany a people on the way."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f4d432ec6a8c2d581fcf", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Paul’s spiritual rock and drink", "note": "The commentary treats water as the literal support of the traveler; Paul rereads Israel’s wilderness drink from the rock as spiritual drink from Christ, adding a Christian interpretation to water on the journey."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c9d7502ce43eee0ba565", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water from the rock on the way", "note": "The commentary says water keeps a traveler’s work standing; Paul recalls Israel drinking from a spiritual rock in the wilderness, making provision on a journey the basis for a Christian reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5108fbb7ee593b0e8cc0", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water from the rock for the journey", "note": "The wilderness people drink from the spiritual rock that follows them. It is a strong counterpart to the commentary's claim that water beside the traveller sustains the journey and its affairs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b3e2d57b1bfac616e6c8", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.36", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seed must die to live", "note": "The commentary connects rain-soaked soil, plant growth, and the raising of the dead; Paul says the seed is not made alive unless it dies, using planted growth to explain resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b890f438ece81416dd4e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.37", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a bare kernel becomes a plant", "note": "The commentary says rain leaves a green trace on earth; Paul distinguishes the bare kernel sown from the body that will grow, making plant emergence a model for resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-16647d7f2ac611950d04", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "what is sown is raised", "note": "The commentary links renewed earth to the resurrection of the dead; Paul explicitly compares a perishable seed sown in the ground with the imperishable body raised."}
{"connection_id": "BC-278927ea85e54d5aaee3", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "planting, watering, divine growth", "note": "Paul plants, Apollos waters, and God gives the growth. Its agricultural distinction makes explicit the commentary's basic figure: water serves growth, yet the giver of growth remains God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3eba1861182895d65296", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass withers and flower falls", "note": "Human flesh is compared to grass that withers and a flower that falls. As an explicit inverse, it helps define the significance of the commentary's exceptional plant that does not wither in summer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d7d806f36d24bdef6a7d", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.2.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "springs without water and driven mist", "note": "The false teachers are compared to waterless springs and mists driven by storm. Its paired failures of well and cloud form a precise inverse to the commentary's full well and productive cloud."}
