{"connection_id": "BC-974148d01c4c0df03e38", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.23.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "men draw and carry well water to David", "note": "David's warriors break through, draw water from the Bethlehem well, and bring it to him. The verse supplies the physical act of drawing and carrying water for a ruler, beside the commentary's pulley, traveller's water, and royal-water wordplay."}
{"connection_id": "BC-426b06c01b5cb8096cf6", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.23.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water valued as life itself", "note": "The commentary treats water as the support of life and rule; David refuses to drink the water because it represents the blood of men who risked their lives to fetch it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6feabd89e0f0f9b8b06c", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "court sits and books open", "note": "The commentary names the Day of Judgment; Daniel sees the court sit and books open, giving an apocalyptic court scene for divine judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-55b052af4a44c5e30614", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a human-like figure approaches the Ancient of Days", "note": "The commentary calls God the sovereign of the judgment day; Daniel sees one like a son of man come before the Ancient of Days in the heavenly court."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9d058cde9c23bbaeb7ee", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "everlasting dominion over peoples", "note": "The commentary calls God mālik and Lord of all worlds; Daniel’s human-like figure receives dominion and a kingdom served by all peoples and nations."}
{"connection_id": "BC-206fd50b8bec0b549588", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "land drinks rain from heaven", "note": "The commentary follows rain into soil and plants; Deuteronomy contrasts a land that drinks rain from heaven with Egypt’s irrigation, making rainfall the land’s source of fertility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-072e785714f40ce258fd", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "land drinks rain from heaven", "note": "The promised land drinks water from rain of the heavens, directly holding together sky, rain, ground, and dependence that the commentary develops under the Arabic sky/cloud naming."}
{"connection_id": "BC-159e3c0f66908d2b8d8b", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic samāʾ and Hebrew šāmayim", "note": "The commentary's source label س م و develops sky, cloud, rain, and plant under samāʾ. Hebrew šāmayim in this verse is a cognate sky term, but the verse keeps heaven and its rain distinct while making the land drink the rain; that difference is informative rather than evidence for identical usage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b94c73f72f45bab358f8", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain yields food crops", "note": "The commentary traces rain to vegetation and provision; Deuteronomy promises rain at its proper season so grain, wine, and oil can grow."}
{"connection_id": "BC-40c211292d5dfc55b505", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain, harvest, grass for animals", "note": "The rain in its season yields grain, wine, oil, and grass for livestock. This is a close biblical analogue to the commentary's sequence from poured water to human food, animal provision, and livelihood."}
{"connection_id": "BC-022094dec8da32f457fb", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.26", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blessing or curse set before the people", "note": "The commentary distinguishes those granted favor from those under wrath or astray; Deuteronomy explicitly sets blessing and curse before Israel in the same chapter that ties rain to obedience and harvest."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0207797ccfa68ba73860", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.27", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "obedience brings blessing", "note": "The commentary asks for the path of those given favor; Deuteronomy says blessing follows listening to the LORD’s commandments."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e58944456af5012da6c5", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.28", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "turning aside brings curse", "note": "The commentary asks not to follow the straying path; Deuteronomy names a curse for disobeying and turning aside to other gods."}
{"connection_id": "BC-017453b7b21dd88c26eb", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.15", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "life and good set against death and evil", "note": "The commentary asks for the straight path and distinguishes its people from those who stray; Moses places life and good before Israel alongside death and evil."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5666fedad1e54ce3d189", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.19", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "choose life rather than death", "note": "The commentary seeks the way of those granted favor, not the way of wrath; Moses calls heaven and earth as witnesses and sets life and death, blessing and curse before the people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-456efbed8559078c436e", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.20", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hold fast to God, who is life", "note": "The commentary asks to be guided on a path that sustains life; Deuteronomy says to love the LORD, listen to his voice, and hold fast to him because he is the people’s life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-084fce5ac49baa4db152", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "teaching falls like rain", "note": "The commentary says rain writes its mark in green growth and asks for guidance; Moses compares his teaching to rain and dew that fall on tender grass, making the same image a vehicle for instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c035e107a0f741404f78", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "possible r-b-b cognate in revivim", "note": "The commentary labels its cloud and tender-plant forms from R-B-B; Deuteronomy uses Hebrew revivim for showers falling on tender grass, a plausible cognate cluster whose exact lexical relationship should be checked in the Hebrew text."}
{"connection_id": "BC-30f90efca0557273700b", "ref": "WLC:Deut.8.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "springs and streams in good land", "note": "The commentary pairs the traveler’s road with water and treats water as the support of livelihood; Deuteronomy describes a land of brooks, springs, and deep waters that sustains its inhabitants."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f70fa9b261cbb38d5f8a", "ref": "WLC:Deut.8.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "brooks, springs, and underground waters", "note": "The good land is defined by brooks, fountains, and depths issuing in valley and hill. It complements the commentary's deep water-filled well and its concern that a water source might withdraw underground."}

